And you're pretty much stuck with all the NPCs, from grid
bugs to dragons, being pretty much the same size, just
because 32x32 pixels isn't a whole lot of room to put in
details.  The original artist did take some steps in that
direction, though, dragons and giants pretty much fill that
32x32 space, while something like a gnome may take up only
10% of the total pixels.

xanthian.

Sadly, the large tiles are not smoothly integrated from a
developers' viewpoint.  The small tiles are stored in their
base format as plain ASCII text, from which appropriate
graphic conversions for each platform's supported graphics
can be automatically generated.  The large tiles, however,
don't have a text base format; they are just artwork, which
makes them a lot less portable between systems.  It would be
a fair programming chore to reproduce the whole toolkit that
makes the small icons work, to also work for the large
icons, and the text files would be, of course, enormous, way
over a megabyte between the file for monsters and the file
for "everything else".

Still, if someone wants to take on the task, that would make
a single style of large icons usable on all platforms, which
would be nice if it isn't the case that at least some large
icons exist for each platform now.



